{"reference":"2 Peter 1","book":"2 Peter","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter holds divine provision and human diligence together. It addresses its readers as sharing a faith of equal honor, says that divine power provides what is needed for life and godliness, and then urges practiced fidelity as they seek to make their calling and election firm. The passage presents diligence as its exhortation, while interpretations of assurance, merit, perseverance, and falling away remain disputed.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 1:1","2 Peter 1:10-11","2 Peter 1:19","2 Peter 1:20","2 Peter 1:3"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_001_e7ab9c70344aa3319888c5a1"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Key Interpretive Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"“Faith of equal honor” resists treating the recipients as spiritually inferior, though exactly whom their faith is compared with is debated. Likewise, becoming “partakers of the divine nature” belongs to the passage’s moral and future-looking setting; it has been important in traditions of participation in God, but it does not erase the distinction between God and creatures or establish a spiritual elite. The virtues in verses 5–7 form cumulative rhetoric, not a tested sequence for measuring people’s worth or ability.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 1:1","2 Peter 1:20-21","2 Peter 1:21","2 Peter 1:4","2 Peter 1:5-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_001_edb07c9c3129335b68fc430b"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"The writer frames the coming death as putting aside a tent and as an exodus, then makes remembrance central to the letter’s final instructions. This resembles a farewell-testament pattern, but that comparison does not settle questions about historical authorship. The promised entrance into the eternal kingdom functions rhetorically to encourage diligence, not as something that can be purchased or controlled by religious leaders.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 1:10-11","2 Peter 1:11","2 Peter 1:12-15","2 Peter 1:13-15","2 Peter 1:4"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_001_ac365f9a3940c5ee35c07e20"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The letter presents its account of the voice on the holy mountain as eyewitness testimony rather than a devised story. That contrast explains the force of the appeal within the letter’s argument, but it does not mean that every later claim to apostolic authority authenticates itself. The image of the morning star in verse 19 may refer to future illumination, Christ, or an inward confirmation; the wording leaves the metaphor open.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 1:1","2 Peter 1:16-18","2 Peter 1:17","2 Peter 1:19"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_001_57a94909a1257e22b132818e"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"The statement that no prophecy of Scripture is of “private interpretation” is debated in context: it may concern prophecy’s origin, its interpretation, or both. In the next verse, prophetic speech is attributed to people speaking from God while carried by the Holy Spirit. These lines ground divine agency, but do not by themselves settle later questions about dictation, inerrancy, a closed canon, or a modern prophetic office.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 1:1","2 Peter 1:20-21","2 Peter 1:21","2 Peter 1:4","2 Peter 1:5-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_001_edb07c9c3129335b68fc430b"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Literary Design","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The letter repeatedly emphasizes remembrance: its readers already know the truth, yet the writer intends to keep stirring their memory. The sequence from faith through excellence, knowledge, self-control, endurance, godliness, mutual affection, and love is likewise a deliberate literary pattern. Together these features make the chapter a sustained call to remember and embody what has already been received.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 1:12-15","2 Peter 1:5-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_001_a89941808a868dc74759350e"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why It Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"The promises and divine power in verses 3–4 ground the chapter’s exhortation, but they do not promise health, wealth, control, or exemption from ordinary care. The appeal to prophecy also calls for testing claims through context, evidence, and accountable community; it cannot rightly be used to forbid study and questions or to shield leaders from scrutiny.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 1:19-21","2 Peter 1:3-4"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_001_1e539e6b77acb59c9857addf"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading the Surrounding Letter","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"Interpretation and application of the letter’s surrounding passages must reject antisemitism, supersessionism, dehumanizing opponents, misogyny, anti-LGBTQ coercion, nationalism, colonial mission, forced conversion, religious violence, prosperity extraction, and ecological neglect.","verse_refs":["2 Peter 2:1-22","2 Peter 3:1-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_peter_001_ecc93d2de22464bf147e0d2d"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"inference"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}